```

The CLI and MCP accept the brief as unknown JSON, and this mutation stores it without structural validation. Existing UI code assumes `brief.answers` exists, so importing `"brief": "invalid"` can persist state that later crashes expressions such as `brief?.answers.goal`. Use a validator matching `ProjectBrief`. Severity 3, confidence 0.98.

2. `convex/integrations.ts:419` — citation fields remain unchecked before being stored.

```ts
    entries: v.array(v.any()),
```

```ts
      const entry = { ...raw, folioId } as Record<string, unknown>;
      if (typeof entry.title !== "string" || !entry.title.trim()) {
        throw new Error("Each citation needs a title");
      }
```

Only `title`, `id`, and `accessedAt` are narrowed; fields such as `url`, `author`, and `doi` can be numbers or objects. The CLI parser similarly casts after checking only `title`. Persisting `{ "title": "x", "url": 7 }` succeeds, while the citations UI calls `entry.url.replace(...)`, causing a runtime `TypeError`. Validate the full citation shape. Severity 3, confidence 0.99.

3. `packages/twyne-tools/src/client.ts:87` — the SDK’s typed return contract is based on an unchecked JSON cast.

```ts
    let body: IntegrationSuccess<T> | IntegrationFailure | undefined;
    try {
      body = text ? (JSON.parse(text) as IntegrationSuccess<T> | IntegrationFailure) : undefined;
```

```ts
    return body.data;
```

Checking only `body.ok === true` does not establish that `data` is `T`. A malformed or schema-drifted response such as `{"ok":true,"data":null}` is returned as `Folio[]`, after which CLI callers use `.length` and iteration and can crash. Per-operation response validation is needed at this network boundary. Severity 2, confidence 0.92.

